WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.694 --> 00:00:19.107
Hi, everyone! It's me, Melissa, again. Today in science class, we started a fun engineering project.

2
00:00:19.108 --> 00:00:23.968
We built towers, using just Jell-O, straws, and cardboard.

3
00:00:23.969 --> 00:00:30.188
Tomorrow we're going to shake our towers to see which ones can withstand the force of earthquakes.

4
00:00:30.189 --> 00:00:34.274
Designing a strong tower was definitely pretty tricky.

5
00:00:34.275 --> 00:00:41.843
It reminded me of a landmark we learned about in Mr. Fay's class this week: the Golden Gate Bridge in San Francisco, California.

6
00:00:41.844 --> 00:00:45.667
The Golden Gate is one of the most famous bridges in the world.

7
00:00:45.668 --> 00:00:53.324
But before its construction began, lots of people thought it would be impossible to build. I'll explain why in a minute.

8
00:00:53.325 --> 00:00:58.187
First I have to tell you a little bit about the city's geography.

9
00:00:58.188 --> 00:01:07.411
San Francisco sits on a small peninsula between the Pacific Ocean and the San Francisco Bay, which are connected by the Golden Gate strait.

10
00:01:07.412 --> 00:01:16.809
By the early twentieth century, the city had become very crowded, and city officials wanted to make the trip across the strait easier for city residents.

11
00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:20.972
For years a ferry service had taken people back and forth.

12
00:01:20.973 --> 00:01:27.152
But by the early twentieth century, the ferries were very crowded, with long lines.

13
00:01:27.153 --> 00:01:31.714
Of course, the obvious solution was to build a suspension bridge across the water.

14
00:01:31.715 --> 00:01:36.482
That's a bridge with a roadway held up by towers and heavy-duty cables.

15
00:01:36.483 --> 00:01:44.314
In San Francisco there was just one problem: the Golden Gate strait is a terrible place to build a bridge!

16
00:01:44.315 --> 00:01:54.207
Strong winds blow through the strait, and waters flowing down from the mountains collide with the incoming tide, creating turbulent currents and dense fog.

17
00:01:54.208 --> 00:02:02.882
Plus, the Golden Gate Strait sits close to a major fault line where earthquakes can occur. It sounds pretty dangerous, right?

18
00:02:02.883 --> 00:02:08.610
Still, by 1916 city officials were determined to forge ahead.

19
00:02:08.611 --> 00:02:15.276
A bridge builder named Joseph Strauss was hired, and he began working with a team of engineers and designers.

20
00:02:15.277 --> 00:02:23.674
His vision was to create a bridge that could handle the harsh conditions in the strait and yet also serve as an image of beauty.

21
00:02:23.675 --> 00:02:32.263
Construction began in 1933, right in the middle of the Great Depression, when many people were unemployed and desperate to find work.

22
00:02:32.264 --> 00:02:38.991
Ironworkers from all over the country arrived in San Francisco, hoping to get a job working on the bridge.

23
00:02:38.992 --> 00:02:43.172
Even people just pretending to be ironworkers arrived!

24
00:02:43.173 --> 00:02:49.575
Workers had to deal with the strait's nasty weather, slippery surfaces, and dizzying heights.

25
00:02:49.576 --> 00:02:58.322
On top of all that, they were using dangerous materials like extremely hot rivets and air hammers, which required special skill to operate.

26
00:02:58.323 --> 00:03:02.495
From the start, Strauss was very concerned about his workers' safety.

27
00:03:02.495 --> 00:03:10.540
The Golden Gate Bridge was the first major construction project where workers wore hard hats, a new invention at the time.

28
00:03:10.541 --> 00:03:17.814
Strauss also insisted that everyone hook themselves to safety ropes, firing anyone who refused to do so.

29
00:03:17.838 --> 00:03:20.871
And he had a huge net placed under the bridge.

30
00:03:20.871 --> 00:03:29.061
During the four years of construction, 19 workers fell into the net—that's 19 lives that were saved!

31
00:03:29.062 --> 00:03:34.535
However, construction of the Golden Gate Bridge didn't go entirely smoothly.

32
00:03:34.536 --> 00:03:43.536
A trestle being used to access one of the towers was destroyed twice, first by a ship lost in the fog, and then by strong winds.

33
00:03:43.537 --> 00:03:51.774
And despite all the safety precautions, 11 workers died. For the first three years, only one man was killed.

34
00:03:51.775 --> 00:03:59.768
Then in February 1937, a large piece of scaffolding fell, killing ten more men.

35
00:03:59.769 --> 00:04:07.918
Bridges are usually painted gray or silver, but the Golden Gate Bridge needed a color that would stand out in the thick San Francisco fog.

36
00:04:07.919 --> 00:04:13.521
Mr. Fay said the U.S. Navy wanted to paint the bridge with black and yellow stripes.

37
00:04:13.522 --> 00:04:21.076
Even though that might have looked cool, I think international orange, which was developed just for the bridge, is perfect.

38
00:04:21.077 --> 00:04:27.654
It's visible in the fog, and it looks beautiful against the blue water and surrounding green and brown hills.

39
00:04:27.655 --> 00:04:31.404
By May of 1937, the bridge was complete.

40
00:04:31.405 --> 00:04:43.576
Workers had used more than 75,000,000 kilograms of steel, 129,000 kilometers of cable, and nearly 298,000 cubic meters of concrete.

41
00:04:43.577 --> 00:04:49.733
At the time the Golden Gate Bridge was the longest and tallest suspension bridge in the world.

42
00:04:49.734 --> 00:04:58.481
On opening day pedestrians, runners, people on unicycles, and even on stilts, showed up to celebrate and travel across the bridge.

43
00:04:58.482 --> 00:05:02.962
I was surprised to learn that the bridge is still being worked on all the time.

44
00:05:02.963 --> 00:05:07.466
The fog and salty ocean air constantly eat away the bridge's steel.

45
00:05:07.467 --> 00:05:16.618
So a full-time crew of ironworkers and painters work year round, painting exposed areas and replacing corroded steel and rivets.

46
00:05:16.619 --> 00:05:24.366
Those workers frequently have to walk up the cables, high above the traffic. I definitely wouldn't want that job!

47
00:05:24.367 --> 00:05:31.445
I would, however, love to visit the bridge. Several companies offer tours of the bridge and surrounding area.

48
00:05:31.446 --> 00:05:42.686
Tourists can see a statue of Strauss, a giant cross section of suspension cable from the bridge, and a plaque memorializing the 11 workers who died during its construction.

49
00:05:42.687 --> 00:05:49.686
There are other cool landmarks to see in the area too—including Alcatraz Island, the site of a former prison.

50
00:05:49.687 --> 00:05:57.089
From 1934 to 1963, some of America's most notorious criminals were sent to Alcatraz.

51
00:05:57.090 --> 00:06:05.003
Today tourists can walk around the island, get a glimpse of prison life, and learn about some daring escape attempts.

52
00:06:05.004 --> 00:06:09.738
To celebrate the bridge's opening, Joseph Strauss, wrote a poem about it.

53
00:06:09.739 --> 00:06:17.994
Mr. Fay recited the poem, and now whenever I see pictures of the majestic bridge spanning the strait, I think of Strauss' own words:

54
00:06:17.995 --> 00:06:24.317
Its great steel arms link shore with shore, Its towers pierce the sky.

55
00:06:24.318 --> 00:06:31.213
Well, I'm sure my Jell-O tower won't last seventy-five years like the steel arms of the Golden Gate Bridge.

56
00:06:31.214 --> 00:06:37.960
But I hope it will at least survive tomorrow's earthquake test. Wish me luck!
